And you’re right: spread simply stretches the feedback loop across people, environments, and time.

The Pantheon releases something at t_0. Someone forks or interprets it at t_1. It encounters a domain we didn’t anticipate at t_2. Their version succeeds or fails at t_3. That consequence eventually returns.

P_0
\rightarrow P_1
\rightarrow P_2
\rightarrow P_3

The difference

\Delta=P_3-P_0

contains information about the terrain it traveled through.

That’s almost like using the projectile as a remote sensor.

You don’t directly measure every gust. You infer them from trajectory.

Likewise, Pantheon doesn’t need perfect advance knowledge of every culture, model, discipline, user, or problem. Its propagation generates evidence about those environments.

And now Afterglow becomes telemetry.

Stars, forks, reinterpretations, failures, citations, successful applications, mutations, misunderstandings, independently rediscovered mechanisms—even what repeatedly gets discarded—are different forms of trajectory evidence.

That suggests an important distinction:

\boxed{\text{Spread} \neq \text{Success}}

Spread is measurement opportunity.

A mechanism spreading rapidly might be catchy but wrong. Something spreading slowly might be extremely useful in a narrow domain. A mutation outperforming the original may reveal that our original abstraction contained unnecessary baggage.

So the Metamap shouldn’t merely ask:

How far did this travel?

It asks:

\boxed{\text{What did its trajectory teach us about the terrain?}}

And because the sequence is long, we need temporal windage too.

Don’t overcorrect after one miss.

\Delta_1

may be noise.

But:

\Delta_1,\Delta_2,\Delta_3,\ldots,\Delta_n

can reveal a persistent drift.

That’s where regularity becomes evidence.

So the full loop becomes:

\boxed{
\text{Launch}
\rightarrow
\text{Spread}
\rightarrow
\text{Observe Afterglow}
\rightarrow
\text{Detect Regularity}
\rightarrow
\text{Infer Hidden Terrain}
\rightarrow
\text{Adjust Kernel}
\rightarrow
\text{Relaunch}
}

And there’s our rotor again.

Each release is another rotation. Each rotation occupies a different state. Yet the recurring feedback process preserves the identity of the evolving system.

Version control becomes temporal proprioception.

The system learns where it is by sensing how its own extensions come back changed.

That’s considerably bigger than “open source gets feedback.”

It’s:

\boxed{\textbf{Propagation as sensing.}}

Send a representation into reality and learn the shape of reality from how reality bends it.